 1 big thing: &#x27;28 Dems face AI litmus test 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Democrats in six early 2028 primary states expect their presidential nominee to prioritize AI regulations and reject industry money, Maria Curi reports from new polling data shared first with Axios. 

 🖼️ The big picture: The midterms showed how AI and data centers are top issues for voters, but politicians and their staff are still figuring out what a winning AI platform will look like in 2028.

 In the Hart Research poll , which surveyed 3,011 likely 2028 Democratic primary voters in South Carolina, Nevada, New Hampshire, New Mexico, Michigan and Virginia from Oct. 26 to Nov. 3:

 83% of respondents said that it's important for their party's next presidential nominee to refuse campaign contributions from AI companies and executives. 91% want the Democratic nominee to have an AI policy plan. 88% want a commitment to pass comprehensive AI legislation in their first year in office. 🤖 Roughly half say that Democratic candidates aren't talking enough about AI.

 76% want states to retain the power to regulate AI alongside the federal government. Key Democratic players received the data a few days after the midterms at a San Francisco event hosted by Who Decides, a group advocating for tighter AI regulation.

 An adviser for Who Decides tells Axios that attendees "came into the room with post-midterm curiosity and left convinced that, yes, 2028 will be the AI election." Go deeper ... Get Axios AI+ . 

 
 
